{"batchcomplete":"","query":{"normalized":[{"from":"Coffee_Dripper","to":"Coffee Dripper"},{"from":"Darkstone_Periapt","to":"Darkstone Periapt"},{"from":"Wing_Boots","to":"Wing Boots"},{"from":"Tough_Bandages","to":"Tough Bandages"},{"from":"Burning_Blood","to":"Burning Blood"},{"from":"Mark_of_Pain","to":"Mark of Pain"},{"from":"Potion_Belt","to":"Potion Belt"},{"from":"Data_Disk","to":"Data Disk"},{"from":"Nilry's_Codex","to":"Nilry's Codex"},{"from":"Runic_Dome","to":"Runic Dome"},{"from":"Peace_Pipe","to":"Peace Pipe"},{"from":"Nuclear_Battery","to":"Nuclear Battery"},{"from":"Gambling_Chip","to":"Gambling Chip"},{"from":"Captain's_Wheel","to":"Captain's Wheel"},{"from":"Violet_Lotus","to":"Violet Lotus"}],"pages":{"1307":{"pageid":1307,"ns":0,"title":"Akabeko","revisions":[{"revid":56309,"parentid":56024,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation||1}} {{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Akabeko\n| Image       = Akabeko.png\n| BetaImage   = Akabeko Old.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Description = Your first {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attack}} each combat deals 8 additional damage\n| Flavor      = Muuu~\n| GivesToPlayer = Vigor\n}} {{Power Infobox|Vigor}}\n{{R|Akabeko}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. Its damage buff is applied as 8 stacks of {{BD|Vigor}} at the start of combat.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Akabeko}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Akabeko}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Akabeko}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Akabeko}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Akabeko}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Akabeko}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Akabeko}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Akabeko}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Akabeko}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Akabeko}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\nWhen calculating damage dealt, all the additive bonuses are added first, and then multiplicative bonuses are multiplied. The final result is rounded down. {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} {{BD|Vigor}} is an additive bonus.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Wreath of Flame}} and {{R|Akabeko}} both use {{BD|Vigor}}, and therefore the effect is stack-able (additive).\n* {{BD|Strength}} stacks additively with {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} {{BD|Vigor}}.\n* {{KW|Wrath}} or {{KW|Divinity}} {{KW|Stance|Stances}}, {{BD|Vulnerable}} and {{C|Phantasmal Killer}}'s {{BD|Double Damage}} stack multiplicatively with {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} {{BD|Vigor}}.\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that deal damage to all enemies (such as {{C|Cleave}} and {{C|Conclude}}) will apply the damage bonus to all affected enemies.\n* {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} {{BD|Vigor}} will work on every hit of the first multi-hit {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} card played:\n** {{C|Pummel}} triggers 4 (5) times for a total of 40 (50) damage.\n** {{C|Sword Boomerang}} triggers 3 (4) times for a total of 33 (44) damage.\n** {{C|Twin Strike}} triggers 2 times for a total of 26 (30) damage.\n** {{C|Whirlwind}} triggers X times for a total of 13X (16X) damage to all enemies.\n** {{C|Dagger Spray}} triggers 2 times for a total of 24 (28) damage to all enemies.\n** {{C|Eviscerate}} triggers 3 times for a total of 45 (51) damage.\n** {{C|Riddle with Holes}} triggers 5 times, for a total of 55 (60) damage.\n** {{C|Skewer}} triggers X times for a total of 15X (18X) damage.\n** {{C|Barrage}} triggers N times for a total of 12N (14N) damage. (N = number of {{KW|Channel|channeled}} Orbs)\n** {{C|Rip and Tear}} triggers 2 times for a total of 30 (34) damage.\n** {{C|Thunder Strike}} triggers N times for a total of 15N (17N) damage. (N = number of {{KW|Lightning}} Orbs channelled)\n** {{C|Flying Sleeves}} triggers 2 times for a total of 24 (28) damage.\n** {{C|Tantrum}} triggers 3 (4) times for a total of 33 (44) damage.\n** {{C|Ragnarok}} triggers 5 (6) times for a total of 65 (84) damage.\n* {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} {{BD|Vigor}} will count towards additional {{KW|Block}} given by {{C|Wallop}} and additional healing given by {{C|Reaper}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* {{C|Heavy Blade}} only gains a multiplicative effect from {{BD|Strength}} and therefore does not multiply the bonus damage from {{BD|Vigor}}. \n* {{C|Blizzard}} is ''not'' a multi-hit effect, and therefore only benefits from {{BD|Vigor}} once.\n* If an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} is duplicated by {{C|Echo Form}}, {{R|Necronomicon}}, {{C|Double Tap}}, {{C|Omniscience}}, or {{P|Duplication Potion}}, only the first instance will benefit from {{R|Akabeko}}.\n* If played with an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} that deals 0 damage, the {{BD|Vigor}} is consumed, dealing 8 Damage. However, if the player has negative {{BD|Strength}}, the final attack will deal lesser damage. For example:\n** No {{KW|Block}} {{C|Body Slam}} will deal 8 Damage, and with -7 {{BD|Strength}}, will deal 1 Damage.\n** {{C|Riddle with Holes}} with -3 {{BD|Strength}} will deal 8 * 5 damage, and with -4 {{BD|Strength}} deal 7 * 5 Damage.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{M|Nemesis|Nemesis'}} {{BD|Intangible}} and the {{M|Corrupt Heart|Corrupt Heart's}} {{BD|Invincible}} can completely negate Akabeko's effect.\n* {{BD|Weak}} will reduce {{R|Akabeko|Akabeko's}} damage.\n* If played with an {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|attack}} that deals 0 hits (for example, {{C|Barrage}} with 0 channelled Orbs), the {{BD|Vigor}} is consumed, dealing 0 Damage.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* An [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabeko Akabeko] is a legendary cow from the Aizu region of Japan, who inspired a traditional toy that the relic is based off of.\n* Since {{BD|Vigor}} is capped at the integer limit, unlike {{BD|Strength}} which is capped at 999, this can be used to deal the highest possible damage in 1 hit in the game.\n\n== Gallery ==\n<gallery>\nFile:Akabeko Old.png|Old Akabeko Sprite\n</gallery>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Common Relic: Akabeko\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3086":{"pageid":3086,"ns":0,"title":"Astrolabe","revisions":[{"revid":56885,"parentid":50914,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Astrolabe\n|Image       = Astrolabe.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Upon pickup, {{KW|Transform}} 3 cards, then {{KW|Upgrade}} them.\n|Flavor      = A tool to glean invaluable knowledge from the stars.\n|GivesToPlayer = Transform, Upgrade\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Transform}}{{Keyword Infobox|Upgrade}}{{R|Astrolabe}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Astrolabe}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Transform}} effects, {{R|Astrolabe}} can be an effective form of pseudo-card-removal by having a reasonable likelihood of converting low-quality cards into higher quality cards.\n** For example, a random card offered by {{KW|Transform}} effects will often be better than keeping a {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Starter|Starter}} {{C|Strike (Ironclad)|Strike}} or {{C|Defend (Ironclad)|Defend}}. \n* The {{KW|Transform}} effect from {{R|Astrolabe}} does not behave any differently from other {{KW|Transform}} effects (besides upgrading the card afterwards). Therefore, any synergies with {{KW|Transform}} effects will also work with all 3 cards affected by {{R|Astrolabe}}:\n** {{R|Ceramic Fish}} grants 9 [[Gold]] for each {{KW|Transform}} (a total of 27 [[Gold]]).\n** {{KW|Transform|Transforming}} a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} with {{R|Omamori}} active will remove it from the player's deck and consume 1 charge of {{R|Omamori}}.\n** {{KW|Transform|Transforming}} a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} with {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} will grant +6 Max HP for each card (a total of +18 Max HP if all 3 {{KW|Transform|Transforms}} are used on {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}}).\n** {{KW|Transform|Transforming}} a {{C|Parasite}} will cause the player to lose 3 Max HP.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* One or more {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} granted by {{R|Astrolabe}} can be \"wasted\" if a card {{KW|Transform|Transforms}} into a card type affected by one of {{R|Molten Egg}}, {{R|Toxic Egg}}, or {{R|Frozen Egg}} as they would already have been {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} otherwise.\n<!-- What happens if you transform a card into Searing Blow while having Molten Egg? Does it become a +1 or +2? My gut says it should be +2 but I have not tested this. -->\n\n== Trivia ==\n* An astrolabe is an astronomical instrument used to measure various things about space and the sky, acting as a star chart, a physical model of the sky,  an inclinometer (measuring elevation), and an analog calculator for assisting with certain kinds of mathematical problems relating to astronomy.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astrolabe</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Astrolabe relic now works even if your deck contains 3 cards or less.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** Game now saves after receiving cards from Pandora's Box and Astrolabe via Neow so your cards don't vanish.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1449":{"pageid":1449,"ns":0,"title":"Brimstone","revisions":[{"revid":56332,"parentid":55668,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Brimstone\n| Image       = Brimstone.png\n| Rarity      = Shop\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = At the start of your turn, gain 2 {{BD|Strength}} and ALL enemies gain 1 {{BD|Strength}}.\n| Flavor      = Emanates an infernal heat.\n| GivesToPlayer = Strength\n| GivesToPlayer = Strength\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Strength}}\n{{R|Brimstone}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Ironclad]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Brimstone}} can be combined with {{C|Limit Break}} to very quickly gain a lot of {{BD|Strength}}.\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Attack|Attacks}} that hit multiple times are especially effective with {{R|Brimstone}}, such as {{C|Sword Boomerang}}, {{C|Pummel}}, and {{C|Fiend Fire}}.\n* {{C|Heavy Blade}} specifically scales more effectively from {{BD|Strength}} despite not being a multi-hit card. It's damage will ramp up rapidly thanks to {{R|Brimstone|Brimstone's}} {{BD|Strength}} gain.\n* {{C|Reaper}} is an incredibly powerful (and in many cases necessary) card when combined with the rapid {{BD|Strength}} gain of {{R|Brimstone}}. It can be used near the end of a combat, likely after the player has taken significant damage from the {{BD|Strength}}-buffed enemies, to restore a lot of the player's missing HP and allow them to survive long enough to end the fight.\n* Effects that lessen the benefits of {{BD|Strength}} on enemies can be used to counteract the constant buffing of enemies from {{R|Brimstone}}, such as {{C|Disarm}}, sources of {{BD|Weak}} like {{C|Uppercut}} or {{C|Shockwave}}, and {{BD|Intangible}} can all help the player survive longer fights.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* Many enemies (especially [[Bosses]] and [[Elites]]) are fought in multi-enemy combats or have multi-attacks, both of which will make the {{BD|Strength}} buff from {{R|Brimstone}} significantly more dangerous.\n* The {{M|Corrupt Heart|Corrupt Heart's}} {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Blood Shots}} attack is a '''significant''' limiting factor for {{R|Brimstone|Brimstone's}} usability. Dealing with the {{BD|Strength}} gain on this very dangerous multi-attack without one or more of {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Dark Shackles}}, {{C|Impervious}}, {{C|Panic Button}}, a source of {{BD|Weak}} (such as {{C|Uppercut}} or {{C|Shockwave}}), and/or a reliable method of gaining {{BD|Intangible}} will almost certainly cause the player's death right on the cusp of victory.\n** At best, {{M|Corrupt Heart}} will use {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Blood Shots}} on turn 2 (having gained 2 {{BD|Strength}} from {{R|Brimstone}}) which would deal '''4 x 12 for 48''' ('''{{Asc|4|4 x 15 for 60}}''') damage if the player prevents the {{BD|Vulnerable}} from turn 1's {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Debilitate}} in some way, but more likely would deal '''6 x 12 for 72''' ('''{{Asc|4|6 x 15 for 90}}''') damage with the {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n** If {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Echo}} is used on turn 2 instead, then {{Int|Corrupt Heart|Blood Shots}} on turn 3 (having now gained 3 {{BD|Strength}} from {{R|Brimstone}}) would deal '''5 x 12 for 60''' ('''{{Asc|4|5 x 15 for 75}}''') damage without {{BD|Vulnerable}}, or '''7 x 12 for 84''' ('''{{Asc|4|7 x 15 for 105}}''') damage with the {{BD|Vulnerable}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* Brimstone is an old term for [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sulfur sulfur], often used in historic texts and literature.\n* Brimstone means \"burning stone\", coming from the Middle English word ''brennan'' meaning \"to burn\".<ref>https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/1911_Encyclop%C3%A6dia_Britannica/Brimstone</ref>\n* {{R|Brimstone}} is a reference to the [https://bindingofisaacrebirth.wiki.gg/wiki/Brimstone item of the same name from The Binding of Isaac series]. It's iconic symbol from The Binding of Isaac (originating from the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alchemical_symbol#Alchemical_compounds alchemical symbol for black sulfur], referred to as the [https://symbolism.fandom.com/wiki/The_Leviathan_Cross Leviathan Cross]) can be found hidden in the art for {{R|Brimstone}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Brimstone relic buffed. Now gives the player 2 Strength and enemies 1 Strength. (was 1 Strength to the player and 1 Strength to enemies)\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** New Shop Relic: Brimstone.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1439":{"pageid":1439,"ns":0,"title":"Burning Blood","revisions":[{"revid":50796,"parentid":37129,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Burning Blood\n|Image       = BurningBlood.png\n|Rarity      = Starter\n|Character   = Ironclad\n|Description = At the end of combat, heal 6 HP.\n|Flavor      = Your body's own blood burns with an undying rage.\n}}\n{{R|Burning Blood}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Starter|Starter}} [[Relic]] of the [[Ironclad]]; he will always start every new run with it in his possession and it can't be obtained by any other character.\n\nIt can be upgraded into the {{R|Black Blood}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relic}} if it's found in a Boss Chest.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Burning Blood}}'s healing happens at the end of combat ''before'' the combat rewards screen opens, so it still benefits from {{R|Magic Flower}}'s healing buff (healing for 9 HP instead of the normal 6 HP).\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As with all healing effects, {{R|Burning Blood}} restores no HP if the player possesses {{R|Mark of the Bloom}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 6: We're Back!'''\n** Black Blood, Burning Blood, and Meat on the Bone no longer heal you if you are dead.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2571":{"pageid":2571,"ns":0,"title":"Captain's Wheel","revisions":[{"revid":56927,"parentid":50873,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Captain's Wheel\n|Image       = CaptainsWheel.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = At the start of your 3rd turn, gain 18 {{KW|Block}}.\n|Flavor      = Wooden trinket carved with delicate precision. A name is carved into it but the language is foreign.\n|GivesToPlayer = Block\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}{{R|Captain's Wheel}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Captain's Wheel}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Captain's Wheel}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Captain's Wheel}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Captain's Wheel}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Captain's Wheel}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Captain's Wheel}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Captain's Wheel}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Captain's Wheel}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Captain's Wheel}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Captain's Wheel}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Captain's Wheel}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Captain's Wheel}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Some enemies have devastating attacks on turn 3 (such as {{M|Gremlin Nob}} or {{M|Slime Boss}}). {{R|Captain's Wheel}} can be effective at assisting in damage mitigation for these fights with dangerous third turns.\n* The {{KW|Block}} can help delay the activation of {{BD|Buffer}} gained through {{R|Fossilized Helix}} or {{C|Buffer}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Captain's Wheel}} makes it more difficult to trigger {{R|Centennial Puzzle}}, should you want to.\n* The {{KW|Block}} is wasted on enemies that do no damage on the third turn, unless the '''block''' is carried over to the next turn using effects like {{C|Barricade}}, {{C|Blur}}, and {{R|Calipers}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Captain's Wheel}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic in a set of relics which all grant {{KW|Block}} on different turns of combat; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} {{R|Anchor}} on turn 1 and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} {{R|Horn Cleat}} on turn 2.\n* A ship's wheel is a device on a ship that allows the vessel to be steered and change its course.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ship%27s_wheel</ref>\n** While in media and pop culture the captain of a ship is often seen steering the vessel, in reality it is the job of the helmsman to perform the steering. The captain and navigator are responsible for directing the course that the helmsman will maintain. The captain and helmsman may be the same person on smaller vessels with a smaller crew, but ultimately they are different titles/roles and have different responsibilities.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3078":{"pageid":3078,"ns":0,"title":"Coffee Dripper","revisions":[{"revid":56941,"parentid":50920,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Coffee Dripper\n|Image       = CoffeeDripper.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You can no longer Rest at Rest Sites.\n|Flavor      = \"Yes, another cup please. Back to work. Back to work!\" - The Architect\n|GivesToPlayer = Energy\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Coffee Dripper}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Coffee Dripper}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Coffee Dripper}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* The drawback of {{R|Coffee Dripper}} can be lessened by finding alternative forms of healing, such as {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}, {{R|Meat on the Bone}}, {{C|Self Repair}}, {{C|Reaper}}, or {{C|Bite}}.\n** {{R|Eternal Feather}} in particular can provide a pseudo-rest effect whenever the player visits a [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Site]], allowing them to continue using the Smith option since Rest is no longer available.\n* The drawback of {{R|Coffee Dripper}} can be overall negligible if the player's deck is already well-equipped at mitigating damage, such as from effective use of {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, {{R|Torii}}, {{R|Tungsten Rod}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{C|Feel No Pain}}, or {{C|Apparition}}.\n* The other \"Rest Site Relics\", {{R|Girya}}, {{R|Peace Pipe}}, and {{R|Shovel}}, can provide the player with more options at [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]] to help them use these floors effectively when they're unable to Rest.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* The drawback of {{R|Coffee Dripper}} can be quite punishing for decks that struggle to avoid \"chip damage\"; small amounts of damage that the player just barely can't {{KW|Block}} or mitigate in some other way. This may cause the player to die before they can find alternative forms of healing or find ways to mitigate damage more effectively.\n* {{R|Dream Catcher}} is completely negated since the player can no longer Rest at [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]], rendering it useless.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The visual design of {{R|Coffee Dripper}} is directly inspired by the [https://global.hario.com/product/coffee/dripper/VDC.html ceramic Hario V60].<ref>https://www.reddit.com/r/slaythespire/comments/1job9th/even_more_pixelart_relics_29179/mkshagk/</ref>\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 56: The Collector'''\n** Rest option now becomes disabled if you can't Rest (Coffee Dripper relic).\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** Adding boss relic Coffee Dripper.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2372":{"pageid":2372,"ns":0,"title":"Darkstone Periapt","revisions":[{"revid":55727,"parentid":50840,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Darkstone Periapt\n|Image       = DarkstonePeriapt.png\n|Rarity      = Uncommon\n|Description = Whenever you obtain a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}}, increase your Max HP by 6\n|Flavor      = The stone draws power from dark energy, converting it into vitality for the wearer.\n}}\n{{R|Darkstone Periapt}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic can be {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 25% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}}, which can be {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic (which can be {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}), and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n{{R|Darkstone Periapt}} does not retroactively grant Max HP for {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} that are already in the player's deck when the relic is acquired.\n\n=== Ordering ===\n* Max HP is gained immediately if the player obtains a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} during combat, for example when fighting a {{M|Writhing Mass}} that adds a {{C|Parasite}} to the player's deck.\n** This will also trigger the healing bonus from {{R|Magic Flower}}, granting 6 Max HP and restoring 9 HP immediately.\n* Max HP will not be gained from {{C|Curse of the Bell}} if {{R|Darkstone Periapt}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic reward from {{R|Calling Bell}} as the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} is added to the player's deck before they are presented the relics.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Cursed Key}} adds a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} to the player's deck every time they open a non-[[Boss]] [[Chest]].\n* {{R|Necronomicon}} and {{R|Calling Bell}} are both powerful relics that each add a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} to your deck ({{C|Necronomicurse}} and {{C|Curse of the Bell}} respectively).\n* {{R|Blue Candle}} can help mitigate the negative effects of having one or more {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} in the player's deck.\n* The max HP is not lost when a {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curse}} is removed from the player's deck, such as by using the card remove service at a [[Merchant]] or from [[Events]] such as {{E|The Divine Fountain}} or {{E|Purifier}}.\n** {{R|Du-Vu Doll}} may incentevize the player to keep the {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} in their deck to benefit from the {{BD|Strength}} gain, however.\n\n=== Anti Synergies ===\n* {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Curse|Curses}} blocked by {{R|Omamori}} will not trigger the Max HP gain from {{R|Darkstone Periapt}}.\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* In some cultures throughout history, a periapt is a charm worn on a necklace that is used as protection against disease and bad fortune.\n** Can be called a \"nazar\", \"amulet\", \"pendant\", or \"talisman\" in other cultures and contexts.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Darkstone Periapt relic now works with Hoarder mod.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Darkstone Periapt relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 14: Bullet Time'''\n** Darkstone Periapt and War Paint now have flavor descriptions.\n* '''Weekly Patch 4: Full speed ahead!'''\n** Adding new relic Darkstone Periapt.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1926":{"pageid":1926,"ns":0,"title":"Data Disk","revisions":[{"revid":56751,"parentid":55729,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Data Disk\n| Image       = DataDisk.png\n| Rarity      = Common\n| Character   = Defect\n| Description = Start each combat with 1 {{BD|Focus}}.\n| Flavor      = This disk contains precious data on birds and snakes.\n| GivesToPlayer = Focus\n}}\n{{Power Infobox|Focus}}{{R|Data Disk}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Defect|Defect-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Data Disk}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Data Disk}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Data Disk}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Data Disk}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Data Disk}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Data Disk}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Data Disk}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Data Disk}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Data Disk}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Data Disk}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Defect]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all effects that grant {{BD|Focus}}, {{R|Data Disk}} synergises well with any effect that {{KW|Channel}} or {{KW|Evoke}} {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} (except for {{KW|Plasma}}, which is unaffected by {{BD|Focus}}).\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Data Disk}} is at its best when paired with [[Defect]] strategies that rely on {{KW|Orb|Orbs}}. Cards like {{C|Reprogram}} and {{C|Hyperbeam}} that lower {{BD|Focus}} tend to not work well with decks that make good use of {{R|Data Disk}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 27: Hello World'''\n** This week adds the long anticipated third character into the game!\n** Content: 9 Relics.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2492":{"pageid":2492,"ns":0,"title":"Gambling Chip","revisions":[{"revid":50878,"parentid":37168,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Gambling Chip\n|Image       = GamblingChip.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = At the start of each combat, discard any number of cards, then draw that many cards.\n|Flavor      = You can see a small inscription on one side. It reads: \"Bear's Lucky Chip!\"\n|Keywords    = Draw, Discard\n}}\n{{R|Gambling Chip}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. After ''\"start of combat\"'' relics have triggered and the player has drawn their opening hand, they will be prompted to select any amount of cards in their hand to discard. One new card will be drawn for each card discarded in this way. The player can decide to skip discarding any cards and keep their opening hand exactly the way it is, if they prefer. After this has finished, the player's first turn continues as normal.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Gambling Chip}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Gambling Chip}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Gambling Chip}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Gambling Chip}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Gambling Chip}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Gambling Chip}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Gambling Chip}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Gambling Chip}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Gambling Chip}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Gambling Chip}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Gambling Chip}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Gambling Chip}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}} always waits to trigger until after the player has finished drawing their opening hand.\n<!-- Gambling Chip has the same ordering/timing as Warped Tongs, so if you change this section please also check if the change makes sense to be made for Warped Tongs as well! -->\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}} will always trigger ''after'' the following effects have triggered regardless of whether {{R|Gambling Chip}} was acquired before or after them:\n** {{R|Ring of the Snake}} and {{R|Ring of the Serpent}}\n** {{R|Bag of Preparation}}\n** {{R|Snecko Eye}}\n** {{R|Pure Water}} and {{R|Holy Water}} (the {{C|Miracle|Miracle(s)}} are added to the player's hand first, and they can decide to discard any of them)\n** {{R|Bottled Flame}}, {{R|Bottled Lightning}}, and {{R|Bottled Tornado}} (the player can decide to discard the bottled card)\n** {{R|Ninja Scroll}} (the {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} are added to the player's hand first, and they can decide to discard any of them)\n** {{R|Mark of Pain}} (the {{C|Wound|Wounds}} are shuffled into the player's draw pile first, and they may be drawn if any cards are discarded and re-drawn)\n** {{R|Enchiridion}} (the player can decide to discard the free {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} that was added to their hand)\n** {{R|Toolbox}} (the player will be prompted to choose 1 of 3 {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless cards}} first, and can then decide to discard it)\n* {{R|Warped Tongs}} will trigger before or after {{R|Gambling Chip}} depending on their pickup order (i.e. from left to right in the player's relic bar).\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* All of the relics that trigger ''after'' {{R|Gambling Chip}} in the [[#Order|Order]] heading can allow the player to draw deeper into their deck than usual by discarding the extra cards in their opening hand.\n* If {{R|Warped Tongs}} is acquired after {{R|Gambling Chip}}, it will {{KW|Upgrade}} a card in the player's hand after they've finished discarding and re-drawing, and therefore it may have a higher likelihood of {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrading}} a more desirable card.\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}} will trigger [[Keywords#Discard|discard]] synergies such as {{R|Tingsha}} and {{R|Tough Bandages}} since its [[Keywords#Discard|discard]] effect happens during the player's turn.\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}} can allow the player to easily activate {{C|Reflex}} and {{C|Tactician}} if they are drawn in their opening hand.\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}} can allow the player to discard important cards with {{KW|Ethereal}} (such as {{C|Apparition}}) if they are not needed on turn 1 so that they may be used later in the combat.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} from {{R|Ninja Scroll}} can be discarded in order to draw deeper into the player's deck, but they must be careful as they may draw into the low-impact {{C|Shiv|Shivs}} normally later in the fight.\n* The 0-cost {{QueryLink|Cards|type:Power|Power}} from {{R|Enchiridion}} can be discarded in order to draw deeper into the player's deck, but it will be reverted to its normal {{KW|Energy}} cost the next time it's drawn.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Mark of Pain}} will shuffle {{C|Wound|Wounds}} into the player's draw pile before {{R|Gambling Chip}} draws cards. If any cards are discarded and re-drawn, the player may draw into one or more of the {{C|Wound|Wounds}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* A gambling chip (or \"casino chip\", or just \"chip\" when used in context) is a small disc used as currency in casinos instead of legal tender. Money is exchanged for chips upon entering the casino, and they are used to buy-in to the casino's games and given as rewards for winning. The chips are then \"cashed out\" back into money before exiting.<ref>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casino_chip</ref>\n* {{R|Gambling Chip}}'s flavor text mentions ''\"Bear's Lucky Chip!\"'', and is a reference to the enemy {{M|Bear}} from the {{E|Masked Bandits}} event.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 56: The Collector'''\n** Gambling Chip relic now triggers after card-draw based relics such as Ring of Serpent.\n* '''Weekly Patch 36: Power Through'''\n** Draw and Discard piles can now be opened from hand select screen (Gambling Chip, Armaments, etc).\n* '''Weekly Patch 10: Quality of Life'''\n** Discard/Draw panels can be viewed during screens such as Survivor, Headbutt, and Gambling Chip now.\n* '''Day 1 Patch'''\n** Start of combat -> Start of each combat. For Gambling Chip description.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1447":{"pageid":1447,"ns":0,"title":"Mark of Pain","revisions":[{"revid":56333,"parentid":55455,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n| Name        = Mark of Pain\n| Image       = MarkofPain.png\n| Rarity      = Boss\n| Character   = Ironclad\n| Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. At the start of combat, shuffle 2 {{C|Wound|Wounds}} into your draw pile.\n| Flavor      = This brand was used by the northern tribes to signify warriors who had mastered pain in battle.\n| GivesToPlayer = Energy\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{Small Card Infobox|Wound}}{{R|Mark of Pain}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Ironclad|Ironclad-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. The {{C|Wound|Wounds}} are not added directly to the player's deck; they are added to their draw pile ''after'' drawing their 5-card opening hand at the start of combat.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Mark of Pain}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Ironclad]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Mark of Pain}} adds {{C|Wound|Wounds}} to the player's draw pile ''after'' they have drawn their 5-card opening hand.\n* {{R|Mark of Pain}} adds {{C|Wound|Wounds}} in relic pickup order (\"from left to right\"), so if any of the following relics were acquired ''after'' {{R|Mark of Pain}} then it will be possible for them to draw one or more {{C|Wound|Wounds}}:\n** {{R|Bag of Preparation}}\n** {{R|Snecko Eye}}\n** {{R|Ring of the Snake}} and {{R|Ring of the Serpent}} (although it is impossible to have either of these [[Relics]] on [[Ironclad]] through normal gameplay)\n* {{R|Mark of Pain}} will always add {{C|Wound|Wounds}} ''before'' {{R|Gambling Chip}} triggers, regardless of relic pickup order. In other words, it will always be possible for the card draw effect from {{R|Gambling Chip}} to draw one or more {{C|Wound|Wounds}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Mark of Pain}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* {{C|Fire Breathing}} will trigger its damage when a {{C|Wound}} is drawn.\n* {{C|Evolve}} will draw a card when a {{C|Wound}} is drawn, effectively replacing it with another usable card.\n** The {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} {{C|Evolve}}+ will draw 2 cards instead, making {{C|Wound|Wounds}} \"draw positive\" as they effectively replace themselves and then draw 1 additional card.\n* Effects such as {{C|True Grit}}, {{C|Burning Pact}}, {{C|Sever Soul}}, {{C|Second Wind}}, and {{R|Medical Kit}} can {{KW|Exhaust}} the {{C|Wound|Wounds}}, preventing them from being drawn again for the rest of that combat.\n* Alongside effects such as {{C|Dark Embrace}}, {{C|Feel No Pain}}, and {{R|Charon's Ashes}}, the {{C|Wound|Wounds}} can become '{{KW|Exhaust}} fodder' for triggering these effects.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* {{R|Mark of Pain}} will shuffle {{C|Wound|Wounds}} into the player's draw pile before {{R|Gambling Chip}} draws cards. If any cards are discarded and re-drawn, the player may draw into one or more of the {{C|Wound|Wounds}}.\n* {{C|Clash}} cannot be played as long as there is one or more of the {{C|Wound|Wounds}} in hand.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion (12-06-2018)'''\n** Updated relic art for Mark of Pain.\n* '''Weekly Patch 3: Balance Balance Balance'''\n** Mark of Pain wording improvements.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3091":{"pageid":3091,"ns":0,"title":"Melange","revisions":[{"revid":55462,"parentid":50980,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Melange\n|Image       = Melange.png\n|Rarity      = Shop\n|Character   = Watcher\n|Description = Whenever you shuffle your draw pile, {{KW|Scry}} 3.\n|Flavor      = Mysterious sands from an unknown origin. Smells of cinnamon.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Scry}}{{R|Melange}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Watcher|Watcher-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can only be bought from the [[Merchant]]. Only 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Shop|Shop Relic}} is available per [[Shop]], even with {{R|The Courier}}, since it cannot restock Shop Relics.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Watcher]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Melange}} triggers ''after'' the player has finished drawing the cards that caused their draw pile to be shuffled.\n** As an example, imagine the player has 2 cards in their draw pile and then ends their turn. At the start of their next turn, they will draw 2 cards, then shuffle their draw pile, then draw their remaining 3 cards (to complete their 5-card opening hand), and then {{R|Melange}} will trigger its {{KW|Scry}} on whatever is left of their new draw pile.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Efficient card draw effects (such as {{C|Wheel Kick}} and {{C|Scrawl}}) can help trigger {{R|Melange}} more often.\n* {{R|Melange}} will {{KW|Scry}} 5 cards instead of 3 cards if the player possesses {{R|Golden Eye}}.\n* {{R|The Abacus}} will grant 6 {{KW|Block}} at the same time as when {{R|Melange}} triggers.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n<!-- This section is included in every Relic that triggers \"whenever you shuffle your draw pile\". If you make any changes to this list, please update other relics like Sundial and The Abacus! -->\n* Effects that say \"''Shuffle a card into your draw pile.''\" do not actually shuffle the draw pile, instead inserting a card into the draw pile in a random position. Therefore, these effects do '''not''' trigger {{R|Melange}}:\n** {{C|Wild Strike}}\n** {{C|Reckless Charge}}\n** {{C|Evaluate}}\n** {{C|Pray}}\n** {{C|Reach Heaven}}\n** {{C|Study}}\n** {{C|Tantrum}}\n** {{C|Alpha}}\n** {{C|Conjure Blade}}\n** {{C|Chrysalis}}\n** {{C|Metamorphosis}}\n** {{C|Beta}}\n** {{R|Nilry's Codex}}\n\n== Trivia ==\n* This relic references the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melange_(Dune) fictional drug of the same name] from the science-fiction novel series [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dune_(franchise) Dune].\n** Both are described as sand-like substances that smell of cinnamon.\n** It grants a level of heightened sense and prescience, which is thematically similar to {{KW|Scry|Scrying}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Shop Relic: Melange\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3134":{"pageid":3134,"ns":0,"title":"Nilry's Codex","revisions":[{"revid":51017,"parentid":50994,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Nilry's Codex\n|Image       = NilrysCodex.png\n|Rarity      = Event\n|Source      = Cursed Tome\n|Description = At the end of each turn, you may shuffle 1 of 3 random cards to shuffle into your draw pile.\n|Flavor      = Crafted by the infamous game master himself. Said to expand one's mind.\n}}\n{{R|Nilry's Codex}} is an {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Event|Event}} [[Relic]]. After the player ends their turn, they are presented with 3 cards (similar to a normal card reward). They may either choose one of them to add to their draw pile, or skip adding any of the offered cards. As with all card generation effects in combat, any card(s) generated by {{R|Nilry's Codex}} do not persist between combats as they are added to the player's '''draw pile''' and not their '''deck'''.\n\n== Sources ==\nCan only be obtained from {{E|Cursed Tome}} after choosing the {{Frame|Read}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Continue}} -> {{Frame|Take}} options and randomly receiving {{R|Nilry's Codex}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Nilry's Codex}} will present the player with 3 cards at the end of their turn before their hand is discarded.\n* {{R|Nilry's Codex}} adds the card to the player's deck before end-of-turn effects like {{C|Combust}}.\n** This means that, due to the interaction between {{R|Centennial Puzzle}} and {{C|Combust}}, it is possible for the player to draw the card created by {{R|Nilry's Codex}} and immediately discard it.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{C|Master Reality}} will {{KW|Upgrade}} the created card.\n* The ability to have more opportunities to find cards needed to win certain fights can be a '''very''' powerful effect. {{R|Nilry's Codex}} can help the player temporarily curb any weaknesses with their deck by potentially offering them solutions:\n** Damage scaling during longer fights from cards such as {{C|Demon Form}}, {{C|Catalyst}}, {{C|Defragment}}, and {{C|Worship}}.\n** Front-loaded damage from cards such as {{C|Immolate}}, {{C|Skewer}}, {{C|Sunder}}, and {{C|Wheel Kick}}.\n** Damage mitigation from cards such as {{C|Feel No Pain}}, {{C|Piercing Wail}}, {{C|Glacier}}, and {{C|Protect}}.\n** {{KW|Energy}} from cards such as {{C|Bloodletting}}, {{C|Tactician}}, {{C|TURBO}}, and {{KW|Stance}} cards that enter or exit {{KW|Calm}} (like {{C|Fear No Evil}} and {{C|Empty Fist}}).\n** Card draw from cards such as {{C|Battle Trance}}, {{C|Acrobatics}}, {{C|Coolheaded}}, and {{C|Rushdown}}.\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* The cards created by {{R|Nilry's Codex}} will not be {{KW|Upgrade|Upgraded}} by {{R|Molten Egg}}, {{R|Toxic Egg}}, or {{R|Frozen Egg}}. These relics will only {{KW|Upgrade}} cards that are permanently added to the player's deck.\n\n== Excluded Cards ==\nAs with most random card generation effects, {{R|Nilry's Codex}} cannot generate cards that are unobtainable from card rewards or cards that can heal the player. The following cards will never be offered by {{R|Nilry's Codex}}:\n* {{C|Feed}} (healing)\n* {{C|Reaper}} (healing)\n* {{C|Alchemize}} (healing with {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}, {{P|Regen Potion}}, and {{P|Fairy in a Bottle}})\n* {{C|Self Repair}} (healing)\n* {{C|Wish}} (healing by wishing for {{C|Fame and Fortune}} with {{R|Bloody Idol}})\n* {{C|Lesson Learned}} (permanent {{KW|Upgrade}})\n* All {{QueryLink|Cards|color:Colorless|Colorless cards}} ({{R|Nilry's Codex}} will only generate cards of the player character's color and is not affected by {{R|Prismatic Shard}})\n* All {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Special|Special cards}}. These are typically cards that can only be acquired by creating them in combat (such as {{C|Shiv}} from {{C|Blade Dance}}) or acquired exclusively from an [[Event]] (such as {{C|Ritual Dagger}} from {{E|The Nest}}).\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Nilry's Codex}} is a reference to the game [https://www.sirlin.net/codex Codex] by [https://www.sirlin.net/about David Sirlin]; a tabletop deckbuilding game that was one of the inspirations for Slay the Spire. \"Nilry's\" is phonetically similar to \"Sirlin\" when spelled backwards, and it's likely that the \"''infamous game master himself''\" mentioned in the relic's flavor text is also a reference to David Sirlin.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V1.1: The Dealer'''\n** Fixed issue if cards are queued and turn is ended after an enemy dies, Nilry's Codex can open and cause issues.\n* '''Weekly Patch 55: Blur'''\n** Draw, Discard, and Exhaust piles can now be viewed during Codex/Discovery card selection.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Fixed issue where viewing relics with Nilrys Codex' popup caused issues with controllers.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** All sources of random temporary card generation in combat (like Dead Branch) can no longer generate cards that provide healing.\n** New tip added to indicate that healing cards cannot be generated randomly during combat.\n* '''Weekly Patch 17: Patched in the Face'''\n** Seeds are now utilized for Enchirdion and Codex.\n* '''Weekly Patch 11: A Library of Features'''\n** Fixed bug where Singing Bowl's +2 HP option appeared for Codex if you opened the map while viewing the cards.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2108":{"pageid":2108,"ns":0,"title":"Nuclear Battery","revisions":[{"revid":55874,"parentid":50947,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Nuclear Battery\n|Image       = NuclearBattery.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Character   = Defect\n|Description = At the start of each combat, {{KW|Channel}} 1 {{KW|Plasma}}.\n|Flavor      = Ooooh...\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Channel}} {{Keyword Infobox|Plasma}}\n{{R|Nuclear Battery}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Defect|Defect-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. \n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Nuclear Battery}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Defect]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n=== Order ===\n* {{R|Nuclear Battery}}, {{R|Cracked Core}}, and {{R|Symbiotic Virus}} all {{KW|Channel}} an {{KW|Orb}} at the start of combat. The order of these {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} is determined by the order the [[Relics]] were acquired in (\"pickup order\"). Since {{R|Cracked Core}} is always the first [[Relic]] acquired (assuming the player still has it), the {{KW|Lightning|Lightning Orb}} will always be {{KW|Channel|Channeled}} first.\n** As there are only three [[Relics]] that {{KW|Channel}} {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} at the start of combat, and [[Defect]] always starts with at least three {{KW|Orb}} Slots, the {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} can never be {{KW|Evoke|Evoked}} before the player's first turn starts.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* The {{Icon|BE}} [[Keywords#Energy|Energy]] generated by the {{KW|Plasma|Plasma Orb}} allows for playing expensive and X cost cards, such as {{C|Echo Form}}, {{C|Meteor Strike}}, {{C|Tempest}}, {{C|Reinforced Body}}, and {{C|Multi-Cast}}.\n\n==== a) General Orb Synergies ====\n* Other {{KW|Orb}} sources, such as {{C|Darkness}} and {{C|Coolheaded}}, can be very helpful for {{KW|Evoke|Evoking}} {{KW|Plasma|Plasma Orbs}} more frequently.\n* Sources of additional {{KW|Orb}} Slots allow for having more [[Orbs#Passive|Orb Passives]] in parallel: {{C|Capacitor}}, {{R|Runic Capacitor}}, {{R|Inserter}}, {{P|Potion of Capacity}}\n* {{C|Compile Driver}} draws a card for each unique type of {{KW|Orb}} {{KW|Channel|Channeled}}.\n* {{C|Barrage}} deals damage for each {{KW|Channel|Channeled}} {{KW|Orb}}, while {{C|Fission}} draws a card and adds 1 {{Icon|BE}} for each {{KW|Channel|Channeled}} {{KW|Orb}}.\n* {{C|Recursion}}, {{C|Dualcast}}, and {{C|Multi-Cast}} each can directly {{KW|Evoke}} your {{KW|Orb|Orbs}} 1, 2, or X times respectively.\n** {{C|Recursion}} can be particularly useful if Nuclear Battery is your only source of {{KW|Plasma}}, allowing you to keep it around longer.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* As {{BD|Focus}} has no effect on {{KW|Plasma|Plasma Orbs}}, {{C|Consume}} removing an {{KW|Orb}} Slot is a strict downside for {{KW|Plasma}}.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3076":{"pageid":3076,"ns":0,"title":"Peace Pipe","revisions":[{"revid":50888,"parentid":37227,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Peace Pipe\n|Image       = PeacePipe.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = You can now remove cards from your deck at Rest Sites.\n|Flavor      = Clears the mind and cleanses the soul.\n|Keywords    = Deck\n}}\n{{R|Peace Pipe}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It adds a \"Toke\" option to [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Rest Sites]], which allows the player to remove one card from their deck.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}} and {{R|Shovel}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Peace Pipe}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Peace Pipe}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Peace Pipe}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Peace Pipe}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Peace Pipe}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Peace Pipe}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Peace Pipe}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Peace Pipe}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Peace Pipe}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Peace Pipe}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Peace Pipe}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Peace Pipe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n* Only '''two''' relics that add an option to [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] ({{R|Girya}}, {{R|Peace Pipe}}, and {{R|Shovel}}) can be offered to the player during a run. Once two have been offered, the third will no longer be accessible by any means.\n* {{R|Black Star}}'s extra relic can never drop a relic that adds an option to [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] (like {{R|Peace Pipe}}).\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n* {{R|Peace Pipe}}'s \"Toke\" options behaves like a normal card remove. It can't be used to remove unremovable cards like {{C|Ascender's Bane}}, {{C|Curse of the Bell}}, and {{C|Necronomicurse}}.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Peace Pipe}}'s '''Toke''' option can provide the player with something to do at [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] if they do not have any particularly good {{KW|Upgrade|Upgrades}} to '''Smith''' and they're comfortable with their amount of HP and don't need to '''Rest'''. Removing unneeded cards (such as {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:strike|basic strikes}} and {{QueryLink|Cards|rarity:Basic&search:defend|basic defends}}) without needing to spend [[Gold]] at a [[Merchant]] for their card removal service can be a good way to use [[Rest Site|Rest Sites]] otherwise.\n** Also applicable if the player has {{R|Fusion Hammer}} and/or {{R|Coffee Dripper}}.\n* {{R|Peace Pipe}} can provide an accessible option for card removal if the player can't afford the card remove service at the [[Merchant]] (e.g. if they have {{R|Ectoplasm}}).\n* {{R|Peace Pipe}} can help thin the player's deck to assist with the '''Minimalist''' [[Achievements|Achievement]].\n\n== Trivia ==\n* [https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/toke Toke] is slang for taking a puff of a marijuana joint or pipe.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Fixed issue that caused Shovel, Girya, and Peace Pipe to incorrectly spawn when they should not.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Peace Pipe relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 30: Skewer'''\n** Peace Pipe relic cannot get rid of Bottled cards anymore.\n* '''Weekly Patch 19: Seed'''\n** Fix for Peace Pipe sometimes causing the wrong effect to happen if you cancel.\n* '''Weekly Patch 8: Ooh Shiny!'''\n** Wording: Campfire -> Rest Site for Girya and Peace Pipe.\n* '''Day 1 Patch'''\n** Toking at a campfire is now cancellable like Smithing.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2529":{"pageid":2529,"ns":0,"title":"Potion Belt","revisions":[{"revid":55479,"parentid":50820,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Potion Belt\n|Image       = PotionBelt.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Upon pickup, gain 2 [[Potions|Potion]] slots.\n|Flavor      = I can hold more Potions using this belt!\n}}\n{{R|Potion Belt}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]]. Immediately on pick up, the player will gain 2 [[Potions|potion]] slots, resulting in a total of 5 ({{Asc|11|4}}) slots instead of the usual 3 ({{Asc|11|2}}).\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Potion Belt}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Potion Belt}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Potion Belt}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Potion Belt}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Potion Belt}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Potion Belt}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Potion Belt}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Potion Belt}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Potion Belt}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Potion Belt}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\nCan only be obtained below floor 49 (the last [[Rest Site]] before the [[Bosses#Act_3_Bosses|Act 3 Boss]]).\n\n== Interactions ==\n* If {{R|Potion Belt}} is removed by {{E|N'loth}}, the player will still have the additional [[Potions|potion]] slots.\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* More potion storage can improve the convenience of storing the greatly increased amount of [[Potions|potions]] generated by {{R|White Beast Statue}} and {{C|Alchemize}}.\n* Greatly increases effectiveness of using {{P|Entropic Brew}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Potion Belt relic no longer spawns beyond floor 48 (excludes Endless mode).\n* '''Weekly Patch 30: Skewer'''\n** Fixed X button icon overlapping floor number in controller mode with Potion Belt.\n* '''Weekly Patch 26: Controller Support Testing'''\n** Entropic Potion now works properly with Potion Belt as intended.\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** New common relic Potion Belt.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3083":{"pageid":3083,"ns":0,"title":"Runic Dome","revisions":[{"revid":55927,"parentid":50930,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Runic Dome\n|Image       = RunicDome.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Description = Gain 1 {{KW|Energy}} at the start of your turn. You can no longer see enemy intents.\n|Flavor      = The runes are indecipherable.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}\n{{R|Runic Dome}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Runic Dome}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* As with all {{KW|Energy}} relics, {{R|Runic Dome}} can allow the player to play high-impact expensive cards more easily.\n* {{C|Go for the Eyes}} will still glow yellow when at least 1 enemy is attacking, giving the player a clue about the enemy intents even though they are no longer visible.\n** {{C|Spot Weakness}}, despite having the same wording as {{C|Go for the Eyes}}, does not have this behavior. It can still be used as an awkward method of seeing whether a specific enemy is attacking based on whether {{BD|Strength}} is gained or not.\n\n==== Predicting Enemy Intents ====\nMany enemies (especially after the enemy moveset changes from {{Asc|17}}, {{Asc|18}}, and {{Asc|19}}) have somewhat predictable attack patterns. A knowledgeable player can use this to their advantage to know (or make an educated guess) what certain enemies are doing on specific turns even though their [[Intent|Intents]] are not visible. For example:\n\n* {{M|Cultist}} will never attack on the first turn and always attack on all subsequent turns. Even its exact damage can be known for certain.\n* {{M|Gremlin Nob}} will always start with {{Int|Gremlin Nob|Bellow}} and not attack on the first turn. Starting from {{Asc|18}}, its attack pattern can be known for certain as it will always follow the repeating cycle of {{Int|Gremlin Nob|Skull Bash}} followed by {{Int|Gremlin Nob|Bull Rush}} twice.\n* Most enemies have percentage-based attacks, so even a knowledgeable player can only make a guess at what these enemies are doing on a given turn. However, there are many attacks which can't be used more than a set number of turns in a row. For example, the {{M|Snake Plant}} has a 65% chance of attacking each turn, but if it attacks 2 turns in a row then it is ''guaranteed'' to use {{Int|Snake Plant|Enfeebling Spores}} on the next turn and not attack the player.\n\nFor more information on the [[Intent|Intents]] of specific enemies, the [[Slay the Spire Wiki/Enemies|Enemies]] page can be used to find more information on any enemy in the game.\n\n==== Dealing With Unknown Intents ==== <!-- There's a better title for this, but I don't know what it is. \"Managing Incorrect Guesses\" is more accurate, but overall feels like a weird title. -->\nEffects which provide consistent damage mitigation without needing input from the player can help reduce the impact of \"incorrect guesses\"; if the player decides to gain less {{KW|Block}} before they know the enemies are attacking for more damage than expected. For example:\n\n* Sources of {{BD|Metallicize}} and {{BD|Plated Armor}} such as {{C|Metallicize}}, {{C|Wish}} (via {{C|Live Forever}}), {{P|Heart of Iron}}, and {{P|Essence of Steel}}.\n* Other forms of passive/automatic {{KW|Block}} gain from effects like {{C|Feel No Pain}}, {{C|Blur}}, {{KW|Frost|Frost Orbs}}, and {{C|Talk to the Hand}}.\n* {{BD|Intangible}} from multiple copies of {{C|Apparition}} or {{C|Wraith Form}}.\n* Debuffs which lower enemy damage for multiple turns, such as {{C|Disarm}}, {{C|Shockwave}}, {{C|Malaise}}, and {{C|Wave of the Hand}}.\n* Healing effects such as {{C|Reaper}}, {{C|Self Repair}}, {{C|Bandage Up}}, {{R|Meat on the Bone}}, and {{R|Toy Ornithopter}}.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* While some enemies have predictable movesets or can sometimes have their next turn guaranteed to be a certain move, most of the time what an enemy does is purely up to chance and there is no real way to deduce their ''exact'' move. Even a knowledgeable player will encounter many scenarios where they cannot know how much {{KW|Block}} they need to mitigate incoming damage, or whether it is a good turn to use cards like {{C|Piercing Wail}} or {{C|Dark Shackles}}.\n* {{C|Spot Weakness}} gives no indication\n\n== Trivia ==\n* The runes in {{R|Runic Dome}} can be seen on the top of {{R|Stone Calendar}}.\n* {{R|Runic Dome}} is part of the \"runic relic set\" alongside {{R|Runic Capacitor}}, {{R|Runic Cube}}, and {{R|Runic Pyramid}}. Unlike most other \"relic sets\", the runic relics have no common mechanic and are purely related by name and visual design.\n* Early in Slay the Spire's development, the [[Intent]] system had not yet been designed so no enemies had any [[Intent|Intents]] to tell the player what they were going to do. The developers added the [[Intent]] system to address the near-universal player feedback about the uncertainty on whether they should play defensively or offensively on a given turn.<ref>https://youtu.be/JFUtfgGuc38?t=523</ref> {{R|Runic Dome}} is a reference to this period of the game's development, and granting the player 1 additional {{KW|Energy}} per turn as compensation for this drawback can hint at the increased difficulty of this early iteration of the game.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 1: The First of Many!'''\n** Adding Relic: Runic Dome (was locked via char 3)\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2534":{"pageid":2534,"ns":0,"title":"Strawberry","revisions":[{"revid":50825,"parentid":37265,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Strawberry\n|Image       = Strawberry.png\n|Rarity      = Common\n|Description = Upon pickup, raise your Max HP by 7.\n|Flavor      = \"Delicious! Haven't seen any of these since the blight.\" - Ranwid\n}}\n{{R|Strawberry}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Small Chest]] or [[Medium Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, {{R|Black Star}}, and {{R|Matryoshka}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} relic can be {{R|Strawberry}}.\n** {{R|Matryoshka}}'s extra relic has a 75% chance of being {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}}, which can be {{R|Strawberry}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Strawberry}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Strawberry}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Strawberry}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Strawberry}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Strawberry}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Strawberry}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Strawberry}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Strawberry}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Neutral ===\n* If {{R|Strawberry}} is removed by {{E|N'loth}}, the player's Max HP will not decrease.\n\n=== Anti-Synergy ===\n* Gaining more Max HP is mostly worthless if the player has {{R|Mark of the Bloom}} which prevents all healing. It also prevents the behavior where gaining Max HP will restore HP equal to the amount of Max HP gained.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Strawberry}} is the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Common|Common}} rarity relic in the \"fruit relic set\" which all increase Max HP on pickup; the others being the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} {{R|Pear}} and the {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} {{R|Mango}}.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 25: Building Blocks'''\n** HP-gain relics (Fruit & Waffle) now have consistent pickup wording.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"3088":{"pageid":3088,"ns":0,"title":"Tough Bandages","revisions":[{"revid":50906,"parentid":37280,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Disambiguation}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Tough Bandages\n|Image       = ToughBandages.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Character   = Silent\n|Description = Whenever you discard a card during your turn, gain 3 {{KW|Block}}.\n|Flavor      = Loss gives strength.\n|Keywords    = Discard\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Block}}\n{{R|Tough Bandages}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Silent|Silent-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]].\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Tough Bandages}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Tough Bandages}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Tough Bandages}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Tough Bandages}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Tough Bandages}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Tough Bandages}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Tough Bandages}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Tough Bandages}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Tough Bandages}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Tough Bandages}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Tough Bandages}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Tough Bandages}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Silent]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* Any cards discarded by {{R|Gambling Chip}} from the player's opening hand will trigger {{R|Tough Bandages}}.\n* {{R|Tingsha}} makes all discards also deal 3 damage to a random enemy.\n* {{C|Calculated Gamble}} discards the player's entire hand and then draws many new cards, allowing for lots of \"free\" {{R|Tough Bandages}} triggers while still having lots of cards to play from their hand.\n** {{C|Storm of Steel}} is similar, but replaces the player's hand with {{C|Shiv|Shivs}}.\n* {{P|Gambler's Brew}} will trigger {{R|Tough Bandages}} as normal for each card discarded.\n\n=== Anti-Synergies ===\n* If only 1 card is discarded and no other {{KW|Block}} effects are used in the same turn, then {{R|Tough Bandages}} will effectively disable {{R|Orichalcum}} for that turn and the player will have 3 fewer {{KW|Block}} than if they had not discarded anything.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Weekly Patch 52: Release Date Price Increase'''\n** Updated relic asset for Tough Bandages.\n* '''Weekly Patch 33: Terror'''\n** Tough Bandages relic is now Rare. (was Uncommon)\n* '''Weekly Patch 29: TURBO'''\n** ToughBandages relics now uses its effect faster (0.35s -> 0.2s).\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"2403":{"pageid":2403,"ns":0,"title":"Violet Lotus","revisions":[{"revid":55995,"parentid":50950,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Violet Lotus\n|Image       = VioletLotus.png\n|Rarity      = Boss\n|Character   = Watcher\n|Description = Whenever you exit {{KW|Calm}}, gain an additional {{KW|Energy}}.\n|Flavor      = The old texts describe that the surface of \"mana pools\" were littered with these flowers.\n}}\n{{Keyword Infobox|Calm}}{{Keyword Infobox|Energy}}{{R|Violet Lotus}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|character:Watcher|Watcher-exclusive}} {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss}} [[Relic]]. It increases the amount of {{Icon|PE}} [[Energy]] gained when exiting the {{KW|Calm}} {{KW|Stance}} by one, from 2 to 3.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be obtained from [[Boss Swap]].\n* After fighting a [[Boss]], {{R|Violet Lotus}} can be offered as a choice of 3 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Boss|Boss Relics}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained by [[Watcher]].\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* [[Watcher]] can often use a strategy referred to as \"{{KW|Stance}} dancing\" (or \"{{KW|Stance}} swapping\") which relies on repeatedly entering and exiting {{KW|Stance|Stances}} multiple times each turn. This often includes entering and exiting {{KW|Calm}} multiple times as well, and {{R|Violet Lotus}} will provide the player with additional {{KW|Energy}} when this happens.\n* {{C|Meditate}} is a particularly useful way to enter {{KW|Calm}} at the end of the player's turn as they can use it to {{KW|Retain}} a card which exits {{KW|Calm}} to be used on their next turn. This can allow them to trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} earlier into their next turn.\n* {{C|Mental Fortress}}, while not directly assisting with triggering {{R|Violet Lotus}}, will grant the player {{KW|Block}} whenever they change {{KW|Stance|Stances}}.\n* {{C|Like Water}}, while not directly assisting with triggering {{R|Violet Lotus}}, can grant the player {{KW|Block}} at the end of their turn if they end it while in {{KW|Calm}}. This can be useful if the player plans to end in {{KW|Calm}} so that they can trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} more easily on their next turn.\n\n==== Entering Calm ====\nEffects which enter {{KW|Calm}} are necessary in order to take advantage of {{R|Violet Lotus|Violet Lotus'}} effect:\n* {{C|Vigilance}}\n* {{C|Tranquility}}\n* {{C|Fear No Evil}}\n* {{C|Inner Peace}}\n* {{C|Meditate}}\n* {{R|Teardrop Locket}}\n* {{P|Stance Potion}} (by choosing {{KW|Calm}})\n\n==== Exiting Calm ====\nEffects that exit the current {{KW|Stance}}, or that switch into a {{KW|Stance}} other than {{KW|Calm}}, are necessary in order to take advantage of {{R|Violet Lotus|Violet Lotus'}} effect:\n* {{C|Eruption}}\n* {{C|Crescendo}}\n* {{C|Empty Body}}\n* {{C|Empty Fist}}\n* {{C|Empty Mind}}\n* {{C|Prostrate}} (can only trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} by building up enough {{BD|Mantra}} to enter {{KW|Divinity}})\n* {{C|Indignation}}\n* {{C|Simmering Fury}} (will only trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} if the player is still in {{KW|Calm}} at the start of their next turn)\n* {{C|Tantrum}}\n* {{C|Worship}} (can only trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} by building up enough {{BD|Mantra}} to enter {{KW|Divinity}})\n* {{C|Blasphemy}}\n* {{C|Devotion}} (can only trigger {{R|Violet Lotus}} by building up enough {{BD|Mantra}} to enter {{KW|Divinity}})\n* {{P|Stance Potion}} (by choosing {{KW|Wrath}}) \n\n== Trivia ==\n* The term \"lotus\" refers to many different kinds of plants in the real world. The visual design for {{R|Violet Lotus}} may indicate that it is a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nelumbo_nucifera ''nelumbo nucifera'' lotus] (a.k.a ''Padma lotus'' or ''Indian lotus'', among many other names); a type of pink lotus flower that is culturally and spiritually significant in Hinduism and Buddhism. Much of the [[Watcher]]'s art and design reference Hinduism and Buddhism, so it's likely that this specific type of lotus did inspire {{R|Violet Lotus}}.\n* {{R|Violet Lotus}} is a reference to the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Lotus_(Magic:_The_Gathering) Black Lotus] card of the collectible card game [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic:_The_Gathering ''Magic: The Gathering'']. The card adds 3 mana to the player's mana pool, which is directly referenced by {{R|Violet Lotus|Violet Lotus'}} effect and flavor text.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** CONTENT: Watcher only, Boss Relic: Violet Lotus\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]},"1810":{"pageid":1810,"ns":0,"title":"Wing Boots","revisions":[{"revid":55606,"parentid":50899,"slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","*":"{{Sequel Rename|Wing Boots|Winged Boots}}\n{{Relic Infobox\n|Name        = Wing Boots\n|Image       = WingedGreaves.png\n|Rarity      = Rare\n|Description = You may ignore paths when choosing the next room to travel to 3 times.\n|Flavor      = Stylish.\n}}\n{{R|Wing Boots}} is a {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} [[Relic]]. It allows the player to select any room on the next floor when picking a [[Map Locations|Map Location]], regardless of whether it's connected to the player's current [[Map Locations|Map Location]] or not. This can be done a maximum of 3 times before the relic becomes unusable.\n\n== Sources ==\n* Can be dropped by fighting [[Elites]], by buying from the [[Merchant]], or found in a [[Medium Chest]] or [[Large Chest]]. See Chest and non-Chest chance at [[Relics#Rarity|Relics > Rarity]].\n* Can be obtained from the relics {{R|Calling Bell}}, {{R|Shovel}}, and {{R|Black Star}}.\n** {{R|Calling Bell}}'s {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic can be {{R|Wing Boots}}.\n* Can be obtained from the following [[Events]]:\n** {{E|We Meet Again!}}: Can give {{R|Wing Boots}} in exchange for either a [[Potion]], some [[Gold]], or a [[Card]].\n** {{E|Wheel of Change}}: Can drop {{R|Wing Boots}} as a prize.\n** {{E|Big Fish}}: Can give {{R|Wing Boots}} along with {{C|Regret}}.\n** {{E|Dead Adventurer}}: Either by choosing {{Frame|Search}} and getting {{R|Wing Boots}} as a reward, or by fighting the [[Elite]] (which can drop {{R|Wing Boots}}).\n** {{E|Scrap Ooze}}: Can give {{R|Wing Boots}} with an X% chance, for increasing amounts of damage and likelihood of success on repeated attempts.\n** {{E|Pleading Vagrant}}: Can give {{R|Wing Boots}} in exchange for either 85 [[Gold]] by choosing {{Frame|Offer Gold}}, or {{C|Shame}} by choosing {{Frame|Rob}}.\n** {{E|The Colosseum}}: The {{Frame|VICTORY}} option drops 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Uncommon|Uncommon}} relic, and 1 {{QueryLink|Relics|rarity:Rare|Rare}} relic (which can be {{R|Wing Boots}}).\n** {{E|The Mausoleum}}: Can give {{R|Wing Boots}} along with {{C|Writhe}}.\n** {{E|Mind Bloom}}: The {{Frame|I Am War}} option can drop {{R|Wing Boots}}.\n** {{E|Mysterious Sphere}}: The {{Frame|Open Sphere}} option can drop {{R|Wing Boots}}.\n\n== Spawn Restrictions ==\n* Can only be obtained below floor 41 (2 floors below the mid-Act [[Mechanics#Chests|Treasure room]] in [[Act 3]]).\n* Can never be dropped when using the [[Custom Mode]] modifiers {{KW|Flight}} or {{KW|Certain Future}}.\n\n== Interactions ==\n\n=== Synergies ===\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} can help take advantage of relics that favor certain types of [[Map Locations]] by allowing the player to path into them more easily:\n** [[Elite]]-focused relics like {{R|Black Star}}, {{R|Preserved Insect}} or {{R|Slaver's Collar}} are more useful when the player can path into more [[Elites]].\n** [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Campfire]] relics like {{R|Girya}}, {{R|Shovel}} and {{R|Peace Pipe}} can be used more frequently if the player can path into more [[Map Locations#Rest Sites|Campfires]].\n** [[Gold]]-based relics like {{R|Membership Card}} and {{R|Smiling Mask}} can be more effective with better access to the [[Merchant]]. {{R|Maw Bank}} may encourage pathing away from the [[Merchant]].\n** [[Event]] relics like {{R|Tiny Chest}} and {{R|Juzu Bracelet}} may encourage the player to visit more [[Events]]. Players may also try to find specific [[Events]] that are good for their particular deck, like {{E|Council of Ghosts}}, {{E|Cursed Tome}}, or {{E|Mind Bloom}}.\n** Even normal combats may become more enticing with relics like {{R|Prayer Wheel}} or {{R|White Beast Statue}}.\n\n== Trivia ==\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} is the only relic that alters the functionality of the Map screen.\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} may be a reference to the winged sandals of the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermes#Iconography Greek God Hermes].\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} may also be a reference to the [https://liquipedia.net/dota2/Boots_of_Travel ''Boots of Travel''] item from Dota 2.\n* {{R|Wing Boots}} is called <code>WingedGreaves</code> in the game files.\n* A character wearing a pair of {{R|Wing Boots}} would beat a character wearing {{R|The Boot}} in a race.\n\n== Update History ==\n* '''Patch V2.0: The Watcher'''\n** Relics which gray out are now grayed out using a shader, rather than a grayscale image.\n* '''Weekly Patch 51: Recursion'''\n** Wing Boot relics were still spawning beyond certain floors when they should not.\n* '''Weekly Patch 50: The Final Act'''\n** Wing Boots relic no longer appers after floor 40 (except with the Endless mod on).\n* '''Weekly Patch 47: Metamorphosis'''\n** Wing Boots relic will no longer spawn on Certain Future daily mod.\n* '''Weekly Patch 44: Chrysalis'''\n** Adding controller support for Wing Boots relic map navigation.\n* '''Weekly patch 42: Reboot'''\n** Adding a used up image for Winged Boots relic.\n* '''Weekly Patch 41: Chill'''\n** New Rare Relic: Wing Boots.\n\n{{RelicNav}}\n[[Category:Relics]]"}}}]}}}}